<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>

    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <IsTestProject>true</IsTestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.0.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.9.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="PublicApiGenerator" Version="11.5.4" />
    <!--
      ApiSurface.cs programs directly against Mono.Cecil's ModuleDefinition/TypeDefinition, not
      only against PublicApiGenerator's own surface - so it needs a direct reference of its own
      rather than relying on the transitive one PublicApiGenerator's nuspec happens to declare.
      Pinned to the exact version PublicApiGenerator 11.5.4 depends on; bump together.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Mono.Cecil" Version="0.11.6" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DocToolkit\DocToolkit.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DocToolkit.Primitives\DocToolkit.Primitives.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DocToolkit.Docx\DocToolkit.Docx.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DocToolkit.Xlsx\DocToolkit.Xlsx.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DocToolkit.Pptx\DocToolkit.Pptx.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DocToolkit.Pdf\DocToolkit.Pdf.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\DocToolkit.Html\DocToolkit.Html.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Fixture PDFs live in this project's own assets/ (not spike/out) because spike/ is
    develop-only reference material that scripts/promote-to-main.sh strips from main - a fixture
    left under spike/out would not exist there, and release.yml builds and tests this project
    against main before publishing. Living with the project that consumes them means they survive
    the strip along with tests/ itself.
    Copied into the test output dir rather than referenced via ".." walking from the test
    assembly location: that walk-up path depth changes with configuration/TFM (bin/Debug/net8.0
    vs bin/Release/net10.0 etc.), so it would be fragile across build configurations. Copying
    puts the fixture next to the test DLL every time, so AppContext.BaseDirectory + a fixed
    relative subpath resolves correctly regardless of the working directory `dotnet test` is
    invoked from.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="assets\result.pdf" Link="Fixtures\result.pdf" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="assets\big.pdf" Link="Fixtures\big.pdf" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A real one-slide PPTX ("Hello {{who}}" in a single text box), generated once with
    ShapeCrawler before that package was removed (see PresentationEditorTests.cs). Building a
    minimal valid PPTX by hand with the raw OpenXml SDK needs presentation/master/layout/theme
    parts, so a committed real fixture is far less boilerplate than hand-rolling one.
  -->
  <!--
    Copied to the output directory rather than found by walking up from the test assembly: that
    walk-up depth changes with configuration and TFM (bin/Debug/net8.0 vs bin/Release/net10.0), so
    it breaks depending on how the tests were invoked. Same reasoning as the fixtures below.
    An intended API change means editing the file HERE and rebuilding, not editing the copy.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="PublicApi\*.approved.txt" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="assets\sample.pptx" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Two REAL Word 97-2003 binary documents, produced by Word itself (verified CFB/OLE2 magic
    d0 cf 11 e0, not a renamed .docx). Hand-building a .doc is not a realistic option - the whole
    question a legacy importer has to answer is whether it copes with files real Word wrote, and a
    minimal hand-rolled CFB would prove nothing about that.

    The PAIR is the point, and neither is redundant:
      legacy.doc          heading + paragraphs + a bold run + a 2x2 table. The table makes Word
                          emit a binary Data stream, so this file CANNOT convert without
                          LegacyDocOptions.AllowContentLoss. It is the blocking case.
      legacy-lossless.doc one line of text, no table, no binary stream - converts with no opt-in.

    Measured 2026-08-16: a table is what triggers the block; bold and headings do not. Without the
    lossless file, every "it refused" assertion would pass just as happily against a converter that
    refused unconditionally.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="assets\legacy.doc" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="assets\legacy-lossless.doc" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A real Excel 97-2003 workbook, saved by Excel itself. It exists to pin a MESSAGE, not a
    capability: ClosedXML reports this file as "File contains corrupted data", which is false and
    sends the caller to check a file that is fine. Measured across 62 such files from a .gov crawl.
    A hand-built compound file would not prove the message appears for a real workbook.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="assets\legacy.xls" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="assets\legacy.ppt" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Documents produced by WORD, not by this library. Every other DOCX fixture here is built by
    DocxEditor.Create - by the code under test - so the suite could only ask "can it read back what
    it wrote?". These ask "can it read what Word wrote", which is where A40 and A41 were found.
      word-plain.docx     headings, bold, a table - the control, and it converts
      word-bullets.docx   a default bulleted list, so numbering.xml carries U+F0B7 (A40)
      word-cyrillic.docx  non-Latin text (A41, still open)
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="assets\word-plain.docx" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="assets\word-bullets.docx" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="assets\word-cyrillic.docx" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Real pages from the govdocs1 .gov crawl, kept whole. Every OTHER fixture in this project is
    built by the code under test, so the suite could only find defects in things it already knew how
    to produce - 1,284 tests were green while HTML to PDF succeeded on 58.6% of real pages.

    Globbed rather than listed, deliberately: a hand-maintained list is the thing that goes stale,
    and dropping a file in is exactly how this set is meant to grow. assets/realworld/README.txt
    carries the provenance and says why they must not be edited.

    The .ppt glob is the same principle applied to a CAPABILITY rather than a defect: nothing here
    can write a legacy binary deck, so a real one is the only way to test reading them at all.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="assets\realworld\*.html" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <Content Include="assets\realworld\*.ppt" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
